OpenLiteSpeed 1.9.3 — initialise HttpSession::m_pAioReq on every OS.

The constructor sets m_pAioReq(NULL) only under
`#if defined(LS_AIO_USE_LINUX_AIO) || IOURING || defined(LS_AIO_USE_AIO)`,
but HttpSession::releaseResources() does `if (m_pAioReq) cancelReleaseAio();`
unconditionally at the end of every request. On macOS (no AIO backend) the
member is whatever the heap held: on macOS 26 it was usually zero, on macOS 15
the server died with SIGSEGV in 10 of 20 runs (HttpSession::releaseResources()
+ 140, the "pointer" being ASCII text), and on the macos-15 CI runner on the
first request. AddressSanitizer pins it deterministically on any macOS:
  SEGV in LsAioReq::isPending() ← HttpSession::cancelReleaseAio()
  ← HttpSession::releaseResources(), x0 = 0xbebebebebebebebe (uninitialised).
Found by rexenv/runtimes' build gate, 4 Oct 2026. Upstreamable as-is.

diff --git a/src/http/httpsession.cpp b/src/http/httpsession.cpp
index ceb73c1..e5077f0 100644
--- a/src/http/httpsession.cpp
+++ b/src/http/httpsession.cpp
@@ -171,9 +171,10 @@ HttpSession::HttpSession()
     , m_iVHostAccess(0)
     , m_lockMtHolder(0)
     , m_pAiosfcb(NULL)
-#if defined(LS_AIO_USE_LINUX_AIO) || IOURING || defined(LS_AIO_USE_AIO)
+    /* Unconditional: releaseResources() tests m_pAioReq on every OS, so on a
+     * build with no AIO backend (macOS, the BSDs) an uninitialised member was
+     * read as a pointer at the end of every request. */
     , m_pAioReq(NULL)
-#endif
     , m_sn(1)
     , m_pReqParser(NULL)
     , m_sessSeq(ls_atomic_add_fetch(&s_m_sessSeq, 1)) // ok to overflow / wrap around
